WHY TWO LICENCES

They are doing two different jobs.

The prose needs to be maximally quotable, translatable and forkable, because a
specification nobody may quote is a brochure, and because a standards body that
later adopts this text must not be blocked by its licence. CC BY 4.0 does that.
ShareAlike would be viral into any document quoting it; NoDerivatives would
forbid forking, which is the point of publishing a draft.

The schemas and tooling need a patent grant, and that is not theoretical in this
field: power beaming is dense with patents on retrodirective arrays, rectenna
topologies and beam-forming control. Apache-2.0 carries an express patent grant
from contributors covering their own contributions. MIT carries none, and a
schema repository with no grant is something corporate legal will block adoption
on.

NO PATENT SEARCH HAS BEEN PERFORMED

No patent search has been performed. No essentiality claim is made. No patent
licence is granted by this repository beyond the express grant Apache-2.0 gives
over contributors' own contributions. It is entirely possible that implementing
parts of this specification requires a licence from a third party. This project
has not investigated that and is not in a position to. Implementers should take
their own advice.

See GOVERNANCE.md, section IPR.

NOTE ON AUTOMATED LICENCE DETECTION

Automated tools generally report a single licence per repository and will pick
one of the two above. Neither answer is complete. This file is the authoritative
statement.
